# How Mobius remembers: the CircularBuffer

## Introduction

Mobius keeps a *fixed* amount of history. Whether a device has been running for a
minute or a month, the memory it spends on metrics and events stays flat. The piece
that makes that possible is [`circular_buffer`](https://hexdocs.pm/circular_buffer),
a tiny fixed-size FIFO: once it is full, every new item pushes out the oldest one.

This notebook explores the library on its own. A companion notebook, **Mobius'
round-robin database**, shows how Mobius stacks four of these buffers to keep
seconds, minutes, hours, and days of history at once.

`CircularBuffer` ships with Nerves Livebook (Mobius depends on it), so there is
nothing to install.

## Creating and filling a buffer

You create a buffer with a maximum size. It starts empty and inserts return a new
buffer (it is an immutable value, not a process).

```elixir
cb = CircularBuffer.new(3)
```

Insert a few items. The `Inspect` output shows the contents oldest-to-newest.

```elixir
cb = Enum.reduce(1..3, cb, fn n, cb -> CircularBuffer.insert(cb, n) end)
```

## Overflow is the whole point

The buffer now holds 3 items, its maximum. Insert a fourth and the oldest (`1`)
falls off the front:

```elixir
cb = CircularBuffer.insert(cb, 4)
```

To see the eviction happen step by step, insert past the capacity and record the
state after each insert. Notice `count` never climbs above 3 and `oldest` keeps
advancing.

```elixir
steps =
  1..7
  |> Enum.scan(CircularBuffer.new(3), fn n, cb -> CircularBuffer.insert(cb, n) end)
  |> Enum.zip(1..7)
  |> Enum.map(fn {cb, inserted} ->
    %{
      inserted: inserted,
      contents: inspect(CircularBuffer.to_list(cb)),
      oldest: CircularBuffer.oldest(cb),
      newest: CircularBuffer.newest(cb),
      count: Enum.count(cb)
    }
  end)

Kino.DataTable.new(steps)
```

## Reading a buffer

A handful of functions cover reading:

```elixir
buffer = Enum.into(1..10, CircularBuffer.new(5))

%{
  to_list: CircularBuffer.to_list(buffer),
  oldest: CircularBuffer.oldest(buffer),
  newest: CircularBuffer.newest(buffer),
  empty?: CircularBuffer.empty?(buffer)
}
```

That `Enum.into/2` works because `CircularBuffer` implements `Collectable`. It also
implements `Enumerable`, so the whole `Enum` toolbox is available, count, sum, map,
and friends, always over the last N items only.

```elixir
{Enum.count(buffer), Enum.sum(buffer), Enum.max(buffer)}
```

## How it stays fast: the two-list trick

A circular buffer needs O(1) inserts. `CircularBuffer` gets that with an
[Okasaki-style queue](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Okasaki%27s_queue): the same
idea behind Erlang's `:queue`. It keeps **two** lists.

The fields are private (the struct is opaque), but peeking at them makes the design
clear:

```elixir
internals = Map.from_struct(Enum.into(1..5, CircularBuffer.new(3)))
```

You will see something like `%{a: [5, 4], b: [3], max_size: 3, count: 3}`:

* `a` is the *input* stack. New items are pushed onto its front, so it is newest-first.
* `b` is the *output* queue, holding older items in order.
* `to_list/1` is simply `b ++ Enum.reverse(a)`, which reconstructs oldest-to-newest.

New inserts just cons onto `a` (O(1)). When `a` needs to become `b`, it is reversed
once and the oldest element is dropped, so the cost of reversing is spread across
many inserts: amortized O(1). The fixed size means an insert into a full buffer is
"add one, drop one", constant memory forever.

## Why Mobius uses it

Two reasons, both about staying bounded on a small device:

* **The event log.** Mobius keeps the most recent events in a `CircularBuffer`, so a
  chatty system can log freely without the log growing without limit.
* **Metric history.** Each time resolution in Mobius' round-robin database is a
  `CircularBuffer`. That is the subject of the next notebook.

Open **Mobius' round-robin database** to see four of these buffers combined into the
history you plot.
